# ML Classification Project: Spam Message Detection

## 1. Introduction

In today's digital world, spam messages are very common and can be annoying or even dangerous. The aim of this project is to build a machine learning model that can automatically classify SMS messages as either Spam or Ham (normal message).

This project demonstrates the complete machine learning workflow, including data preprocessing, feature extraction, model training, evaluation, and model comparison.

---

## 2. Dataset Description

For this project, I used the SMS Spam Collection Dataset.

Dataset Details:

* Total Messages: 5,572
* Classes:

  * Ham (Normal Messages)
  * Spam (Unwanted Messages)

The dataset contains real SMS messages collected for spam classification research.

---

## 3. Data Preprocessing

Before training the model, the text data was cleaned and prepared.

The following preprocessing steps were performed:

* Converted all text into lowercase.
* Removed punctuation marks.
* Removed English stopwords using NLTK.
* Converted text into numerical features using TF-IDF Vectorization.

After preprocessing, the TF-IDF feature matrix size became:

TF-IDF Shape: (5572, 9376)

This means 5,572 messages were converted into 9,376 numerical features.

---

## 4. Train-Test Split

The dataset was divided into training and testing sets.

* Training Data: 4,457 messages
* Testing Data: 1,115 messages

The training data was used to train the models, while the testing data was used to evaluate their performance.

---

## 5. Machine Learning Algorithms Used

To compare performance, two different classification algorithms were used:

### 5.1 Multinomial Naive Bayes

Multinomial Naive Bayes is a probabilistic machine learning algorithm that is widely used for text classification tasks such as spam filtering.

### 5.2 Logistic Regression

Logistic Regression is a supervised learning algorithm that predicts the probability of a message belonging to a particular class.

---

## 6. Model Evaluation Metrics

The following metrics were used to evaluate both models:

* Accuracy
* Precision
* Recall
* F1 Score
* ROC-AUC Score

These metrics provide a better understanding of model performance beyond accuracy alone.

---

## 7. Results

### Naive Bayes Results

* Accuracy: 96.59%
* Precision: 100.00%
* Recall: 74.67%
* F1 Score: 85.50%
* ROC-AUC Score: 98.16%

### Logistic Regression Results

* Accuracy: 94.26%
* Precision: 95.74%
* Recall: 60.00%
* F1 Score: 73.77%
* ROC-AUC Score: 98.70%

---

## 8. Cross Validation

To ensure the models generalize well, 5-Fold Cross Validation was performed.

### Naive Bayes

Cross Validation Scores:

* 0.9677
* 0.9587
* 0.9587
* 0.9614
* 0.9614

Average Accuracy:

96.16%

### Logistic Regression

Cross Validation Scores:

* 0.9552
* 0.9453
* 0.9506
* 0.9452
* 0.9479

Average Accuracy:

94.89%

The cross-validation results show that both models are stable, with Naive Bayes achieving slightly better performance.

---

## 9. Visualizations

The following visualizations were generated during the project:

* Confusion Matrix
* ROC Curve
* Model Comparison Graph

These plots helped in understanding and comparing the performance of both algorithms.

---

## 10. Model Selection

After comparing both models, Naive Bayes was selected as the better model for this project.

Reasons:

* Higher Accuracy
* Higher Recall
* Better F1 Score
* Better Cross Validation Accuracy

Although Logistic Regression achieved a slightly higher ROC-AUC score, Naive Bayes performed better overall for spam message classification.

---

## 11. Testing the Model

The trained model was tested using custom messages entered by the user.

Example:

Message:

"Congratulations! Claim your prize now!"

Prediction:

Spam

Spam Probability:

81.34%

The model was also tested on multiple sample messages and successfully classified most messages correctly.

---

## 12. Conclusion

In this project, I successfully developed a spam message classification system using machine learning techniques.

The project included:

* Data preprocessing
* TF-IDF feature extraction
* Train-test split
* Cross-validation
* Comparison of two machine learning algorithms
* Model evaluation using multiple metrics
* Custom message testing

Based on the experimental results, Naive Bayes performed better than Logistic Regression and was selected as the final model.

This project helped me understand how machine learning can be applied to real-world text classification problems such as spam detection.
